tps://ai.meta.com/research/publications/pixel-seal-adversarial-only-training-for-invisible-image-and-video-watermarking/)\nciteturn6460view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn6458view0\",\"id\":27}); Total lines: 211\nL0: #####\nL1: \nL2: ## Products\nL3: \nL4:   * cite0†Muse L5:   * cite1†Meta AI L6:   * cite2†Media Generation L7:   * cite3†Vibes L8: \nL9: #####\nL10: \nL11: ## AI Research\nL12: \nL13:   * cite4†Overview L14:   * cite5†Projects L15:   * cite6†Resources & Tools L16:   * cite7†Publications L17:   * cite8†GitHub†github.com L18: \nL19: #####\nL20: \nL21: ## Resources\nL22: \nL23:   * cite9†Blog L24:   * cite10†Learning Hub L25:   * cite11†Demos†aidemos.meta.com L26: \nL27: #####\nL28: ## About\nL29: \nL30:   * cite12†Overview L31:   * cite13†Open Source L32:   * cite14†Careers†www.metacareers.com L33: \nL34: * cite15†Products L35: * cite15†AI Research L36: * cite15†Resources L37: * cite15†About L38: * cite16†AI Developers†developer.meta.com L39: * cite17†Try Muse†applink.muse.ai L40: * #### RESEARCH\nL41: \nL42: #### COMPUTER VISION\nL43: \nL44: # Pixel Seal: Adversarial-only training for invisible image and video watermarking\nL45: \nL46: December 18, 2025\nL47: ## Abstract\nL48: Invisible watermarking is essential for tracing the provenance of digital content. However, training state-of-the-art models remains notoriously difficult, with current approaches often struggling to balance robustness against true imperceptibility. This work introduces Pixel Seal, which sets a new state-of-the-art for image and video watermarking.\nL49: We first identify three fundamental issues of existing methods: (i) the reliance on proxy perceptual losses such as MSE and LPIPS that fail to mimic human perception and result in visible watermark artifacts; (ii) the optimization instability caused by conflicting objectives, which necessitates exhaustive hyperparameter tuning; and (iii) reduced robustness and imperceptibility of watermarks when scaling models to high-resolution images and videos.\nL50: To overcome these issues, we first propose an adversarial-only training paradigm that eliminates unreliable pixel-wise imperceptibility losses. Second, we introduce a three-stage training schedule that stabilizes convergence by decoupling robustness and imperceptibility. Third, we address the resolution gap via high-resolution adaptation, employing JND-based attenuation and training-time inference simulation to eliminate upscaling artifacts.\nL51: We thoroughly evaluate the robustness and imperceptibility of Pixel Seal on different image types and across a wide range of transformations, and show clear improvements over the state-of-the-art. We finally demonstrate that the model efficiently adapts to video via temporal watermark pooling, positioning Pixel Seal as a practical and scalable solution for reliable provenance in real-world image and video settings.\nL52: cite18†Download the Paper†scontent-ams2-1.xx.fbcdn.net L53: #### AUTHORS\nL54: \nL55: Written by\nL56: \nL57: Tomáš Souček\nL58: \nL59: Pierre Fernandez\nL60: \nL61: Hady Elsahar\nL62: \nL63: Sylvestre Rebuffi\nL64: \nL65: Valeriu Lacatusu\nL66: \nL67: Tuan Tran\nL68: \nL69: Tom Sander\nL70: \nL71: Alexandre Mourachko\nL72: \nL73: Publisher\nL74: \nL75: arxiv\nL76: \nL77: Research Topics\nL78: \nL79: cite19†Computer Vision L80: \nL81: ### Related Publications\nL82: \nL83: October 14, 2026\nL84: \nL85: #### RESEARCH\nL86: \nL87: #### Human-Guided Agentic AI for End-to-End EHR Research: Sequential Antibody-Drug Conjugate Pathways in Breast Cancer\nL88: \nL89: Arturo Loaiza-Bonilla\nL90: \nL91: October 14, 2026\nL92: \nL93: cite20†Read the Paper L94: \nL95: October 14, 2026\nL96: \nL97: #### RESEARCH\nL98: #### Pickleball injuries, search interest, and participation in the United States, 2014-2023: an ecological time series study\nL99: \nL100: Daniel Kim